* ============================================================================
* 12_cobol_fixed_record_v1.dts
* PDLC COBOL hop -- DBF -> fixed-record -> GnuCOBOL build/run
*
* Status: CANDIDATE / RUNNABLE WHERE GnuCOBOL IS INSTALLED
* Owner: member.derald   authored_by: member.ai.claude.cowork
*
* CORRECTION TO AN EARLIER JUDGEMENT
*   10_crosswalk_cascade_items_v1.dts stage S0 declared the COBOL hop
*   "SKIPPED -- no-preserved-gnucobol-transcript" and left it inert. That
*   wording was inherited from the PDLC review rather than checked, and it
*   understated what exists. Verified in src/edu/edu_cobol.cpp:
*
*     status: supported          -- not experimental
*     COBOL EXPORT STUDENTS      -- writes the fixed-record file from the
*                                   current table (requires logicalName STUDENTS)
*     COBOL BUILD <src.cob>      -- invokes: cobc -x -free   (honours COBCONFIG)
*     COBOL RUN <program>        -- launches the built program
*     COBOL TEST <src.cob>       -- build + run in one step
*
*   And the artifacts are already in the tree:
*     dottalkpp/data/projects/cobol/data/students_ro.dat
*     dottalkpp/data/projects/cobol/src/first_cobol_test.cob
*
*   So the hop was never a capability gap. What was missing is exactly what the
*   review said: a PRESERVED TRANSCRIPT. This script produces one.
*
* PREREQUISITE
*   GnuCOBOL on PATH (cobc). Optionally COBCONFIG pointing at a dialect config.
*   If cobc is absent, BUILD/TEST will fail with a compiler error -- that is a
*   legitimate recorded outcome for a machine without the toolchain, not a
*   defect in this script. Record it and note the host.
* ============================================================================

SET TALK ON
ECHO COBOL BEGIN fixed-record-hop

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* C1  origin: the legacy dBASE III STUDENTS table
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* COBOL EXPORT STUDENTS checks db.logicalName() == "STUDENTS", so the table must
* be opened under that logical name. og/STUDENTS is the 0x03 dBASE III fixture
* used by the dialect ladder -- the same 200 rows feed both proofs.
* PATH PROTOCOL: set the DBF slot, then use a BARE table name. A slashed
* relative path (og/STUDENTS) resolves against the DATA root, not the DBF slot,
* and fails -- that defect sank run 1 of the dialect ladder. Using the bare name
* also matters here for a second reason: COBOL EXPORT STUDENTS checks
* db.logicalName() == "STUDENTS", and the logical name comes from the opened
* table, so it must be opened as STUDENTS.
ECHO COBOL C1 ORIGIN
SETPATH DBF D:\code\ccode\dottalkpp\data\dbf\og
SETPATH
USE STUDENTS
COUNT
STRUCT
ECHO COBOL C1 END

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* C2  DBF -> fixed-record
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* This is the hop the whole PDLC chain starts from, in reverse: fixed-record
* COBOL data is what 1970s-80s systems held, and this proves DotTalk++ can
* still write that shape from a modern table.
ECHO COBOL C2 EXPORT
COBOL EXPORT STUDENTS
ECHO COBOL C2 END

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* C3  compile and run a COBOL program against it
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ECHO COBOL C3 BUILD
COBOL BUILD first_cobol_test.cob
* Bare name, no extension -- the portable, documented form. This exercises the
* BUILD/RUN extension-symmetry fix in run_program_path() (edu_cobol.cpp): on
* Windows RUN now retries once with ".exe" when the bare name has no extension.
* If this line fails with "file not found", the engine has not been rebuilt
* since that fix.
ECHO COBOL C3 RUN
COBOL RUN first_cobol_test
ECHO COBOL C3 END

ECHO COBOL END fixed-record-hop

* ============================================================================
* PASS CRITERIA
*   C1  COUNT = 200, 9 fields, opened from the 0x03 dBASE III fixture
*   C2  EXPORT reports success and writes students_ro.dat under the cobol data
*       root. Record the byte size and record length -- a fixed-record file has
*       a deterministic size, so size / reclen must equal 200 exactly. That
*       division IS the fidelity check for this hop.
*   C3  BUILD completes without compiler error; RUN produces program output.
*
* WHY THE SIZE ARITHMETIC MATTERS
*   Fixed-record formats have no delimiters and no header count. The only proof
*   that 200 rows crossed is that the file is exactly 200 * reclen bytes. A
*   short write is invisible to any tool that just opens the file and reads.
*
* RECORD HONESTLY
*   If cobc is not installed, C3 fails and C1/C2 still stand -- report it as
*   "DBF -> fixed-record proven; compile/run pending toolchain on this host"
*   rather than failing the whole hop. Note the machine and the cobc version
*   (cobc --version) in the transcript header so the proof is attributable.
*
* ============================================================================
* FIRST RECORDED RUN -- 2026-07-26, build Jul 26 2026 12:52:47, v0.6
* Invocation: ./datarun.ps1 -CommandLines 'DOTSCRIPT <abs path>' > tmp\cobol.txt
* Host: Windows, GnuCOBOL present
* ============================================================================
* RESULT: C1 PASS . C2 PASS . C3 BUILD PASS . C3 RUN BLOCKED BY ENGINE DEFECT
*
*   C1  Opened STUDENTS (v32), 200 records, 9 fields, GPA N(4,2), DOB/ENROLL_D D
*
*   C2  "COBOL EXPORT: wrote 200 STUDENTS record(s) to
*        ...\dottalkpp\data\projects\cobol\data\students_ro.dat"
*
*       FIXED-RECORD ARITHMETIC -- THE ACTUAL FIDELITY PROOF:
*         file size      = 22200 bytes
*         record length  =   111 bytes  (the students_ro fixed profile; note it
*                                        is NOT the DBF's 109 -- the fixed
*                                        layout is its own contract)
*         22200 / 111    =   200 records EXACTLY
*       A fixed-record file has no header count and no delimiters, so exact
*       division is the only evidence that all 200 rows crossed. It divides.
*       DBF -> FIXED-RECORD IS PROVEN.
*
*   C3  BUILD PASS -- "COBOL BUILD: created ...\bin\first_cobol_test.exe"
*       GnuCOBOL compiled the program. Only a cosmetic compiler warning:
*         first_cobol_test.cob:47: warning: line not terminated by a newline
*       Worth fixing in the .cob source, but harmless.
*
*       RUN FAILED -- "COBOL RUN: file not found:
*                      ...\bin\first_cobol_test"   (no extension)
*
* ============================================================================
* ENGINE DEFECT -- COBOL BUILD/RUN EXTENSION ASYMMETRY ON WINDOWS
* ============================================================================
*   src/edu/edu_cobol.cpp
*
*     default_build_output_from_source()   (line ~243)
*       #if defined(_WIN32)
*           return cobol_bin_root() / (source_path.stem().string() + ".exe");
*       #else
*           return cobol_bin_root() / source_path.stem();
*       #endif
*
*     run_program_path()                   (line ~412)
*       if (!fs::exists(program_path)) { "COBOL RUN: file not found" ... }
*
*   BUILD is platform-aware and appends .exe on Windows. RUN is NOT -- it does a
*   bare existence check with no .exe fallback. So the documented, natural
*   sequence
*       COBOL BUILD first_cobol_test.cob
*       COBOL RUN   first_cobol_test
*   cannot work on Windows, even though BUILD just succeeded. The user must know
*   to type the extension that BUILD silently added.
*
*   FIXED 2026-07-26 in src/edu/edu_cobol.cpp run_program_path(): under
*   #if defined(_WIN32), when the path does not exist and carries no extension,
*   retry once with ".exe" before reporting not-found. BUILD and RUN are now
*   symmetric across platforms and this script uses the bare name.
*   REQUIRES A REBUILD of dottalkpp before it takes effect.
*
* ============================================================================
* SECOND DEFECT FOUND ON THE SAME RUN -- STALE ASSIGN PATH IN THE FIXTURE
* ============================================================================
*   With the .exe workaround applied, the program RAN and failed deeper:
*     libcob: error: file does not exist (status = 35) for file STUDENT-FILE
*             ('D:' => D:\code\ccode\dottalkpp\data\students_ro.dat)
*
*   first_cobol_test.cob had a hardcoded ASSIGN pointing at the DATA root:
*       ASSIGN TO "D:\code\ccode\dottalkpp\data\students_ro.dat"
*   but COBOL EXPORT STUDENTS writes to
*       ...\dottalkpp\data\projects\cobol\data\students_ro.dat
*   Nothing has ever written to the old location, so OPEN INPUT could never
*   succeed -- the program failed immediately after a successful export.
*   REPAIRED: ASSIGN repointed; the missing trailing newline was also added,
*   which clears the -Wmissing-newline warning on every build.
*
* ============================================================================
* THE COPYBOOK IS THE CROSSWALK -- corrected reading of the arithmetic
* ============================================================================
*   An earlier note here called 111 "the students_ro fixed profile ... NOT the
*   DBF's 109". That was wrong. Reading the FD in first_cobol_test.cob:
*       X(8) X(20) X(15) X(8) X(1) X(4) X(8) X(5) X(40)  =  109 bytes
*   which is EXACTLY the dBASE III record length, field for field:
*       SID N(8,0) LNAME C(20) FNAME C(15) DOB D(8) GENDER C(1)
*       MAJOR C(4) ENROLL_D D(8) GPA N(4,2) EMAIL C(40)
*   So 22,200 = 200 x 111 where 111 = 109 + CRLF under LINE SEQUENTIAL.
*   The COBOL copybook and the DBF schema are the same layout. That equivalence
*   is the migration evidence, not just a size coincidence.
*
* ============================================================================
* RETRACTION -- A FALSE FINDING THIS SESSION PRODUCED (recorded 2026-07-26)
* ============================================================================
*   Gate 4's guard reported "staged PDF predates the assembly it evidences by
*   3h35m". THAT FINDING WAS FALSE and has been retracted.
*
*   The guard compared datetime.fromtimestamp(mtime) -- NAIVE, holding LOCAL
*   time -- against build_utc parsed with the "Z" stripped, also naive. On a
*   UTC-7 host that made the PDF look 7 hours older than it was.
*     PDF mtime, true UTC : 2026-07-23T07:39:07Z
*     assembly build_utc  : 2026-07-23T04:14:23Z
*   The PDF is 3h24m45s AFTER the assembly -- the CORRECT order. There was no
*   provenance violation. Acting on the finding would have meant re-rendering a
*   PDF that was fine.
*
*   Same defect class as session_coordinator.py _age_min(), found the same day,
*   in code written hours apart. Timestamps crossing a process boundary must be
*   epochs or carry a timezone; naive local compared to UTC lies silently and
*   in a plausible direction. FIXED in manual_guard_v1.py parse_iso().
*
*   A guard that invents a violation is worse than no guard. Gate 4 is now
*   FAIL 0 / WARN 4, and all four remaining warnings are real.
*
* ============================================================================
* THIRD DEFECT, SELF-INFLICTED -- FIXED-FORMAT COMMENTS IN A FREE-FORMAT SOURCE
* ============================================================================
*   The repair above was written with fixed-format comments ('*' in column 7).
*   The engine compiles with "cobc -x -free" (edu_cobol.cpp:376,384) -- FREE
*   format, where '*' is not a comment marker and '*>' is. That turned a clean
*   build into ~60 syntax errors: "PROCEDURE DIVISION header missing",
*   "'bytes' is a reserved word", "'STUDENT-FILE' is not defined".
*   The compile flag had already been read while tracing the toolchain; it was
*   simply not applied. Corrected: all comments now use '*>'.
*   Signal missed: the original source carried ZERO comments. In free format a
*   comment needs a marker the author may not have bothered with -- the absence
*   was evidence about the format, not an invitation to document.
*
* ============================================================================
* FINAL RUN -- 2026-07-26, rebuilt engine (edu_cobol.cpp .exe fallback applied)
* ============================================================================
* RESULT: FULL PASS. THE COBOL HOP IS CLOSED END TO END.
*
*   C3 BUILD  "created ...\bin\first_cobol_test.exe"
*             No compiler warnings -- the -Wmissing-newline warning is gone.
*
*   C3 RUN    "COBOL RUN first_cobol_test"   -- BARE NAME, no extension.
*             The .exe fallback in run_program_path() resolved it. BUILD and
*             RUN are symmetric on Windows again; the documented sequence works.
*
*   PROGRAM OUTPUT: 200 student lines, then
*             RECORDS READ: 0000200
*             COBOL RUN: exit code 0
*
*   IDENTITY ACROSS THE HOP -- record 1, read by COBOL from the fixed-record
*   file, against the same record read by DotTalk++ from the dBASE III table:
*             50000000  Taylor  Quinn  2.97
*             SID 50000000 . LNAME Taylor . FNAME Quinn . GPA 2.97
*   Identical. GPA kept both decimals through DBF -> fixed-record -> COBOL READ.
*
* STATUS OF THE COBOL HOP AFTER THIS RUN
*   DBF -> fixed-record ....... PROVEN (200 records, 22,200 = 200 x 111)
*   GnuCOBOL compile .......... PROVEN (clean, no warnings)
*   program execution ......... PROVEN (200 records read, exit 0)
*   copybook <-> DBF schema ... PROVEN EQUIVALENT (109 bytes, field for field)
*
*   The PDLC review's "runtime-capable, proof incomplete" was right about the
*   gap and wrong about the cause: the missing piece was never GnuCOBOL. It was
*   two latent defects -- a BUILD/RUN extension asymmetry in the engine and a
*   stale ASSIGN path in the fixture -- neither of which could surface until
*   somebody actually ran the hop and preserved the output.
*
*   THE LINE NOW READS: fixed-record COBOL hop -- EMPIRICALLY DEMONSTRATED,
*   preserved transcript, 200 records, values identical at both ends.
* ============================================================================
